"Uses nested circles to expose hierarchical containment while sizing leaf circles by nonnegative quantities.\n\nUse when: Hierarchical grouping should be visually prominent and compact enclosure is useful. Approximate leaf magnitude comparisons are sufficient.\n\nAvoid when: Readers must compare parent totals accurately through enclosing circle areas.\n\nVisual grammar: Leaf circle area encodes the leaf value. Nested enclosure encodes hierarchy; unused space inside parents is packing overhead.\n\nBuild: Build a rooted hierarchy with additive leaf values. Scale leaf radii by square root of value and pack siblings without overlap. Add consistent separation and place labels only where they fit within circles.\n\nCheck: Check leaf area ratios match value ratios. Verify every circle is contained within its actual parent. State that parent enclosure area includes packing gaps and is not an exact total encoding.\n\nPitfalls: Parent circles cannot be compared as exact additive areas because packing introduces empty space. Use a treemap or icicle chart when accurate hierarchical share comparison matters most.\n\nExample: Show synthetic document collections as nested circles, sizing leaves by document count and labeling only the larger collections.\n\nAlternatives: Treemap, Sunburst chart, Icicle chart."
